# D4 foodMed — Material Changes Identified During Verification (2026-04-29)

This section documents temporal changes in law, regulation, program, or institutional state identified during the verification pass between the second-pass draft date (2026-04-28/29) and the verification execution date (2026-04-29). The four-field format (what it was / what it is now / when it changed / what it affects) supports downstream temporal-change visualization and cross-domain handoff continuity.

<a id="mc-v-1"></a>
## MC-V-1 — DEA Marijuana Schedule III effective date correction

**What it was (in second-pass OUTPUT):** Today-effective DEA Final Order placing FDA-approved + state-licensed medical marijuana → Schedule III; effective April 29, 2026 (= execution date); 60-day priority window through June 22, 2026.

**What it is now (verified):** DEA Final Order **effective April 28, 2026** (Federal Register publication date; FR Doc. 2026-08176; Vol. 91 No. 81). Pursuant to **Trump EO 14370** signed December 18, 2025 (Increasing Medical Marijuana and Cannabidiol Research). DEA Medical Marijuana Dispensary Registration Portal opens **April 29, 2026 at 9:00 AM EST** (= execution date) with **annual application fee $794**. **60-day priority window through approximately June 27, 2026** (60 days from FR publication). **Adult-use cannabis remains Schedule I**; only FDA-approved products + state-licensed medical cannabis rescheduled. **Synthetically derived THC** (delta-10) excluded. New DEA hearing on broader rescheduling: **June 29 - July 15, 2026** (recess July 3, reconvene July 6). Prior NPRM and hearing **withdrawn April 22, 2026** (FR Doc. 2026-08178; replaces 89 FR 70148). DOJ Final Order issued **April 23, 2026** (Acting Attorney General; Order No. 6753-2026). Section 280E deduction disallowance lifted for state-licensed medical cannabis effective immediately.

**When it changed:** April 22-28, 2026 (DOJ Final Order to FR publication).

**Affects:** SD6 §10 TCT; SD6 §11 G1-SD6-06; F4-T2P2-SD6-2 verification flag (now retrievable starting today).

*Sources:* [S-01](#s-01), [flag for URL-retrieval pass: EO 14370 (December 18, 2025); FR Doc. 2026-08178 (prior NPRM withdrawal April 22, 2026); DOJ Final Order No. 6753-2026 (April 23, 2026)]

<a id="mc-v-2"></a>
## MC-V-2 — ACIP charter rewrite post-court-block

**What it was (in second-pass OUTPUT):** Vaccine policy in active federal litigation; March 2026 federal court block restoring prior schedule pending litigation; prior schedule operative as of execution April 29, 2026.

**What it is now (verified):** Court ruling specific date: **March 16, 2026**, by **U.S. District Judge Brian E. Murphy** (D. Mass.). Plaintiffs: AAP, IDSA, APHA, ACP, Society for Maternal-Fetal Medicine, Massachusetts pediatric chapters, three Jane Doe individuals. Court found ACIP appointments violated **Federal Advisory Committee Act**. Court halted: 13 new ACIP appointments + all post-June 2025 votes (including hep B newborn + COVID downgrade) + government memo enacting new schedule. Schedule reduction was **17 → 11 diseases** (not 18 → 11 as in some early reporting — corrected). **NEW since draft**: **April 9, 2026** — HHS rewrote ACIP charter broadening scope to include "novel vaccine platforms such as mRNA vaccines" + reviewing schedules of other countries (CNN reporting). HHS spokesperson Andrew Nixon called it "routine statutory requirements." Additional context: August 2025 — HHS canceled ~$500M in federal mRNA research funding.

**When it changed:** March 16, 2026 (court ruling); April 9, 2026 (charter rewrite).

**Affects:** SD4 §10 TCT; SD4 §11 G1-SD4-02; F4-T2P2-SD4-2 verification flag.

*Sources:* [S-07](#s-07), [S-37](#s-37), [S-41](#s-41), [S-57](#s-57), [S-58](#s-58), [S-59](#s-59), [flag for URL-retrieval pass: HHS cancellation of ~$500M federal mRNA research funding (August 2025)]

<a id="mc-v-3"></a>
## MC-V-3 — FSIS NRTE breaded stuffed chicken verification activities indefinitely delayed (December 2025; additional withdrawal beyond April 2025 framework)

**What it was (in second-pass OUTPUT):** April 25, 2025 Salmonella Framework for Raw Poultry Products withdrawn (90 FR 17344); January 14, 2026 FSIS public meeting "exploring practical strategies" status flagged; replacement pathway pending.

**What it is now (verified):** Confirmed: April 25, 2025 framework withdrawal (90 FR 17344). **NEW finding**: **December 2025** — FSIS indefinitely delayed sampling and verification activities for the April 2024 finalized determination declaring Salmonella an adulterant in **NRTE (not-ready-to-eat) breaded and stuffed chicken products at >1 CFU/g** (rationale cited: "limitations in available test methods"; disputed by Consumer Reports senior food scientist Dr. Michael Hansen who notes that FSIS's own qPCR method bioMérieux GENE-UP QUANT is validated for 1 CFU/g). January 14, 2026 public meeting held (Docket No. FSIS-2025-0179); USDA Secretary Brooke Rollins + new Under Secretary Dr. Mindy Brashears (sworn same day) attended; comment period extended to **March 4, 2026** (from original February 2, 2026). FSIS sampling data confirms cheaper-cut exposure pathway: **Salmonella detection 4.14% chicken carcasses / 7.62% chicken parts / 24.2% comminuted chicken** (2022 data). New leadership: Dr. Hany Sidrak = FSIS Chief Public Health Veterinarian.

**When it changed:** December 2025 (NRTE delay); January 14, 2026 (public meeting + Brashears sworn in); comment period extended through March 4, 2026.

**Affects:** SD2 §10 TCT; SD2 §11 G1-SD2-02 (Salmonella regulatory pathway); F4-T2P2-SD2-1 verification flag (cheaper-cut exposure pathway data verified at 24.2% comminuted detection rate).

*Sources:* [S-02](#s-02), [S-03](#s-03), [S-13](#s-13)

