## §2. Hierarchical Authority Chain Diagram

```
CONSTITUTIONAL FOUNDATION
  Commerce Clause (Art. I § 8 cl. 3) → authorizes federal regulation of food, drug, cosmetic
    interstate commerce
  Spending Clause (Art. I § 8 cl. 1) → authorizes federal nutrition assistance conditioning
    SNAP / WIC / NSLP

FEDERAL STATUTORY LAYER
  Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act of 1938 (FFDCA) Chapter IV — Foods
    21 U.S.C. §§ 341-350l
    → adulteration / misbranding / labeling / standards of identity
    → STATUTORY STABILITY: HIGH — foundational; bipartisan amendments since 1938
  Food Safety Modernization Act of 2011 (FSMA)
    21 U.S.C. § 350g et seq.
    → Preventive Controls; Produce Safety; FSVP; intentional adulteration rules
    → STATUTORY STABILITY: HIGH — bipartisan implementation through 2022 final rules
  Dietary Supplement Health and Education Act of 1994 (DSHEA)
    21 U.S.C. § 321(ff); § 343-2; § 350b
    → defines supplements; pre-market notification (NDI); adverse-event reporting
    → STATUTORY STABILITY: HIGH — long-stable framework; supplement industry alignment
  FALCPA (2004) + FASTER Act (2021) — major-allergen labeling
    → Big 9 allergens including sesame
    → STATUTORY STABILITY: HIGH
  Modernization of Cosmetics Regulation Act of 2022 (MoCRA)
    21 U.S.C. § 364 et seq.
    → facility registration; product listing; safety substantiation; adverse-event reporting; recall
    → STATUTORY STABILITY: HIGH (2022 enactment) — implementation lag through 2026-2028
  SNAP (Food and Nutrition Act of 2008) / WIC (CNA) / NSLP (RNA 1946)
    7 U.S.C. § 2011 et seq.; 42 U.S.C. § 1786; 42 U.S.C. § 1751 et seq.
    → benefit; categorical eligibility; retailer authorization; school meal architecture
    → STATUTORY STABILITY: MODERATE-HIGH — Farm Bill cycle reauthorization

FEDERAL AGENCY LAYER
  FDA Human Foods Program / Office of Inspections and Investigations (post-July 1, 2024)
    → Philadelphia District at Mellon Independence Center, 900 US Customhouse,
      2nd & Chestnut, Philadelphia, PA 19106 (verify F4-T1-XC-1 P2)
    → primary PA-3-relevant function: HFP foods regulatory oversight; OII inspections
    → ADMINISTRATIVE VULNERABILITY: HIGH — HFP/OII reorganization scope post-July 1, 2024;
      April 2025 FDA RIF (~3,500 RIF; ~20% workforce loss through early 2026); MoCRA
      implementation lag through 2026-2028; per-Center staffing actuals flagged
      F4-T2P2-SD7-1 P1
  USDA Food and Nutrition Service (SNAP / WIC / NSLP administration)
    → Mid-Atlantic Regional Office; PADHS state administration
    → ADMINISTRATIVE VULNERABILITY: MODERATE-HIGH — April 2025 USDA workforce
      reductions (11,300+ deferred resignations effective)

STATE STATUTORY LAYER
  PA Food Safety Act (Pennsylvania Food Code) (3 P.S. § 5701 et seq.)
    → state-level food-code adoption; coordination with PADA
    → relationship to federal: state plan with federal alignment
  PA Department of Agriculture Code (3 P.S. § 401 et seq.)
    → retail / processing / dairy / shellfish

STATE AGENCY LAYER
  PA Department of Agriculture (PADA)
    → Bureau of Food Safety and Laboratory Services; regional office Lancaster
    → primary PA-3-relevant function: retail food licensing oversight; processing
    → ADMINISTRATIVE VULNERABILITY: MODERATE — state capacity stable
  PA Department of Human Services (PADHS)
    → SNAP state administration; PADHS county assistance offices
    → ADMINISTRATIVE VULNERABILITY: MODERATE — administrative capacity stable

LOCAL STATUTORY LAYER
  Philadelphia Code Title 6 Chapter 6-300 (Food Establishments)
    → retail food licensing; food handler certification; inspection regime
    → Home Rule basis: Pennsylvania First Class City Home Rule Act

LOCAL AGENCY LAYER
  PDPH Office of Food Protection (OFP)
    → 1101 Market Street, Philadelphia, PA 19107
    → primary PA-3-relevant function: retail food licensing + routine inspection +
      complaint-driven inspection at retail food establishments
    → ADMINISTRATIVE VULNERABILITY: MODERATE — local capacity stable; sub-area
      inspection density variation flagged F4-T2P2-SD1-2 P2
  PDPH Get Healthy Philly + Healthy Corner Store Initiative + Food Bucks
    → service-provider anchor at retail-access intervention layer
    → ANCHOR ENGAGEMENT SCORING: HIGH at local layer

PA-3 CONSTITUENT LEVEL (aggregate categories per Standard 13)
  SNAP-using households at ~130% FPL across four sub-areas
    → SNAP redemption pattern at retailer-type by sub-area
  Black women PA-3 cosmetics consumers across four sub-areas
    → hair-product chemical-exposure pathway under MoCRA implementation lag
  PA-3 retail food establishments seeking PDPH OFP licensure across sub-areas
    → independent corner stores + small carnicerías + small dietary supplement
      retailers + small / Black-owned cosmetics manufacturers (XC-11)
  PA-3 dietary supplement consumers
    → DSHEA pre-market notification + adverse-event-reporting interface
```

