## §3. Supporting prose

The SD1 chain shows two distinct constitutional pathways operating simultaneously: Commerce Clause regulation of the food / supplement / cosmetics product stream (FFDCA Chapter IV / FSMA / DSHEA / FALCPA-FASTER / MoCRA) and Spending Clause conditioning of nutrition assistance flows (SNAP / WIC / NSLP) [D]. The architecture is comprehensive in nominal coverage; the federal capacity to operate it has eroded in 2025-2026 through the April 2025 FDA RIF (~3,500 RIF) and the April 2025 USDA workforce reductions (11,300+ deferred resignations) [D].

The administrative-vulnerability pattern at the federal layer (HIGH for FDA HFP/OII; MODERATE-HIGH for USDA FNS) operates against state-layer (PADA + PADHS at MODERATE) and local-layer (PDPH OFP at MODERATE) more stable capacity [SD; premise: state and local agencies report no comparable workforce reductions in 2025-2026]. The asymmetry means PA-3-specific food regulatory operations depend increasingly on the more stable state and local layers for retail-level enforcement, while the federal product-level pathway erodes [SD].

The anchor institution at the local layer is PDPH OFP plus PDPH Get Healthy Philly's structural-actor extensions (Healthy Corner Store Initiative; Food Bucks) [D]. Engagement scoring is HIGH at the local layer through structural-actor function: Get Healthy Philly's Healthy Corner Store Initiative places PDPH inside the corner-store retail layer where SNAP redemption concentrates in compound-disadvantage sub-areas, functioning as a local-layer remediation pathway against the corner-store unit-price premium [D]. The federal-layer engagement is HIGH at the regulated-entity dimension (FDA Philadelphia District inspections) but reduced at the structural-actor dimension post-RIF [SD].

The constituent level (per Standard 13) is aggregate. The hierarchy ends at stated categories — no individual is named anywhere in the diagram — and the pathway tracing from constituent back through the hierarchy is what §6 develops.

---

## §4. Statistical profile

National baseline: USDA ERS FY24 reported 13.5% U.S. household food insecurity (47.4 million people in food-insecure households) [D, Source 14]. **The USDA ERS Household Food Security report series was terminated with the 2024 edition** [D, Source 14] — the federal data series characterizing the pattern is itself rolled back going forward.

Pennsylvania state baseline: PA household food insecurity ~10.6% (USDA ERS pre-termination); PA SNAP enrollment ~1.85 million (PADHS; ~14.3% state population) [D, Sources 14, 22].

Philadelphia city baseline: 21.2% Greater Philadelphia food insecurity (Greater Philadelphia FI baseline) [D, Source 8]; >2x the U.S. average. Black Philadelphia FI estimated 28%+ [SD, Source 8 + ACS demographic baseline].

PA-3 specific: 36.9% household food insecurity in studied North Philadelphia tracts (Drexel-Dornsife School of Public Health study; representative of compound-disadvantage North/Northwest Philadelphia Core) [D, Source 9]; SNAP redemption volume per PADHS data at PA-3-aggregated level approximately ~$X million annually flagged for direct retrieval (F4-T2P2-SD1-1 P1).

Federal program flow into PA-3: SNAP FY25 maximum benefit ~$8.05/person/day (USDA FNS published; calculated from $292 maximum monthly for 1-person household × 12 ÷ 365) [D, Source 1]; corner-store unit-price premium 10-30% above supermarket at SNAP-authorized retailers [D, Source 9]; combined pattern — for a SNAP-using PA-3 household of 4 at maximum, $32.20/day nominal × corner-store-only access reduces effective purchasing power to ~$22.50-$29.30/day-equivalent. Post-2021 27% food cost inflation [D, Source 11] compounds the gap.

Food retail infrastructure in PA-3: SNAP-authorized retailer count by sub-area at retrieval target F4-T2P2-SD1-1 P1; ratio of SNAP-authorized supermarkets vs. corner stores varies by sub-area in pattern consistent with documented compound-disadvantage geography [SD; premise: Healthy Corner Store Initiative coverage maps].

PDPH OFP regulatory activity: ~12,000 retail food licenses citywide (PDPH OFP annual; 2024 baseline) [D, Source 5]; routine inspection cadence 6-12 months; sub-area inspection density variation flagged F4-T2P2-SD1-2 P2.

Cosmetics consumer baseline: PA-3 Black women cosmetics consumers — hair-product chemical exposure literature peer-reviewed retrieval target F4-T2P2-SD1-3 P1 [SI; premise: documented over-representation of para-phenylenediamine, formaldehyde-releasing preservatives, parabens, phthalates in products marketed to Black women per broader literature].

---

## §5. Geographic variation across four sub-areas

**North/Northwest Philadelphia Core** [D]: highest documented household FI concentration in PA-3 (36.9% in studied tracts per Source 9); highest SNAP enrollment density; corner-store concentration with limited supermarket access; PDPH OFP routine inspection density flagged for direct retrieval.

**West Philadelphia Core (Cobbs Creek-area)** [D]: significant FI concentration; SNAP-authorized retailer mix shifted post-Rite-Aid pharmacy desert (cross-ref SD3); 52nd Street corridor includes halal-density retail (cross-ref SD2).

**Northwest Philadelphia (Mt. Airy)** [SD]: lower documented FI; mixed-income demographic; kosher-density retail (cross-ref SD2); supermarket access more available.

**South/Southwest Philadelphia** [D]: significant FI concentration; Italian Market + Vietnamese + Mexican carnicería + halal corridors with small / independent retail concentration (XC-11 application — small carnicerías and small ethnic-market retail are SD1 + SD2 boundary actors with PA Food Safety Act + Title 6 §6-300 + Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional interface).

Sub-area summary: documented FI elevation concentrates in North/Northwest Philadelphia Core + South/Southwest Philadelphia + portions of West Philadelphia Core; documented FI lower concentration in Northwest Philadelphia [D]. Cross-sub-area observation: corner-store unit-price premium pattern operates across all four sub-areas but compounds with FI concentration where SNAP-only retail-access geography is most concentrated [SD; premise: Healthy Corner Store Initiative coverage + ACS retailer density].

Sub-area-disaggregation feasibility: F4-T2P2-SD1-1 P1 retrieval attempted in second pass; if PADHS data does not directly disaggregate to PA-3 sub-area, the [SD] inference posture is preserved with explicit premise statement (G1-SD1-06 deepening).

---

## §6. Aggregate Pathway Analysis

**Pathway 1 — SNAP-using PA-3 household at 130% FPL in North/Northwest Philadelphia Core relying on SNAP for primary food access** [D + SD pathway].

Triggering event: monthly SNAP redemption at corner-store retailer absent supermarket access at sub-area level. Institutional steps: SNAP eligibility certified at PADHS county assistance office; EBT card issued; monthly benefit deposited; redemption at SNAP-authorized retailer of household choice within physical-access geography. Decision point: retailer-type selection constrained by physical access; corner-store concentration in N./Northwest Phila Core means redemption at corner store is the operational default.

Illustrative calculation [D rates × stated category]: SNAP FY25 max ~$8.05/person/day × household of 4 = $32.20/day nominal benefit; corner-store unit-price premium 10-30% above supermarket [Source 9] reduces effective purchasing power to ~$22.50-$29.30/day-equivalent in supermarket-pricing terms. Post-2021 27% food cost inflation [Source 11] compounds the gap. **The arithmetic shows the SNAP benefit-vs-need gap is structural, not a function of individual household choice — it reflects the documented retail-geography + benefit-rate + price-premium intersection.**

Documented breakdown patterns: corner-store inventory mix favors shelf-stable processed product over fresh produce; PDPH Healthy Corner Store Initiative places intervention infrastructure but does not eliminate the unit-price premium. Community organization placement: PDPH Get Healthy Philly Food Bucks doubles SNAP value at participating farmers markets (operates structurally against the unit-price premium); coverage by sub-area at deepening target.

**Pathway 2 — Black PA-3 woman cosmetics consumer using hair-products with chemical-exposure profile not yet covered by phased MoCRA implementation** [D + SI pathway].

Triggering event: regular hair-product use with chemical-exposure profile. Institutional steps: cosmetics manufacturer registration + product listing under MoCRA (phased through 2026-2028) [D]; safety substantiation requirement at facility level; adverse-event reporting interface. Decision point: MoCRA's phased implementation means current product inventory at retail predates final regulations; consumer encounters product produced under pre-MoCRA regulatory regime through implementation-lag period.

Illustrative reference: peer-reviewed literature documents over-representation of para-phenylenediamine, formaldehyde-releasing preservatives, parabens, phthalates, and other chemicals concentrated in products marketed to Black women [SI; F4-T2P2-SD1-3 P1 deepening target — promotion to [D] via peer-reviewed retrieval]. MoCRA implementation timeline (final regulations 2026-2028) defines the remediation-availability window. **XC-11 application: small / Black-owned cosmetics manufacturers under MoCRA exemption thresholds may serve PA-3 Black women cosmetics consumers more directly than conglomerated-brand products do; whether MoCRA's small-business exemption thresholds reach these manufacturers is structurally consequential to remediation reach** — flagged for verification.

**Pathway 3 — PA-3 retail food establishment seeking PDPH OFP licensure (independent corner store / small dietary supplement retailer / small cosmetics retailer)** [D pathway].

Triggering event: opening or renewing retail food / dietary supplement / cosmetics retail. Institutional steps: PDPH OFP plan review; construction inspection; operational inspection; license fee; food handler certification; routine inspection at 6-12 month interval. Decision point: PDPH OFP inspection density variation by sub-area [SD; F4-T2P2-SD1-2 P2 retrieval target].

XC-11 application: small / independent retailers carry compliance burden differential vs. corporate-chain retailers; the PDPH Healthy Corner Store Initiative provides structural-actor support against the differential at participating retailers. PA Food Safety Act + Title 6 Chapter 6-300 + Title 6 §6-301 (USDA jurisdictional carve-out at retail per SD2) jurisdictional interface particularly affects small carnicerías and small ethnic-market retail.

**Pathway 4 — PA-3 dietary supplement consumer encountering DSHEA pre-market notification regime** [SD pathway].

Triggering event: dietary supplement purchase and use. Institutional steps: DSHEA pre-market notification (NDI for new ingredients); adverse-event reporting; FDA enforcement post-marketing only [D]. Decision point: DSHEA's structurally light pre-market regime relative to FFDCA Chapter V drugs means consumer encounters product inventory whose safety profile rests substantially on manufacturer / industry compliance. Cross-ref XC-11: small dietary supplement retailers carry retailer-side burden under DSHEA + FALCPA labeling; small / Black-owned supplement manufacturers may operate within or outside FDA enforcement priority zones [SD].

---

## §7. Conversational Narrative Note

**What the SD1 architecture does to PA-3 residents in stated categories — central insight.** The federal SD1 architecture characterizes itself as comprehensive: FFDCA Chapter IV's product safety regime; FSMA's preventive-controls framework; DSHEA's supplement architecture; MoCRA's cosmetics modernization; SNAP / WIC / NSLP's nutrition-assistance Spending Clause flow. The architecture's nominal coverage is not in dispute. What is happening in 2025-2026 is the simultaneous erosion of the *federal data infrastructure* characterizing the very pattern the regulatory architecture would address (USDA ERS Household Food Security report series terminated with the 2024 edition; CDC FoodNet PA-3 disaggregation absence) [D]. What the federal government can no longer see, the federal regulatory architecture can no longer respond to. For SNAP-using PA-3 households at 130% FPL in North/Northwest Philadelphia Core sub-areas with documented 36.9% household FI in studied tracts [Source 9], the rollback removes the visibility — not the pattern.

**The MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence at SD1 layer.** Three documented patterns co-occur at SD1 level: rule-availability erosion (MoCRA implementation lag through 2026-2028 means the federal cosmetics modernization tool remains incompletely operative through the implementation window when remediation for hair-product chemical exposure for Black PA-3 women would be operationally available); federal capacity erosion (April 2025 FDA RIF ~3,500; ~20% workforce loss through early 2026; HFP/OII reorganization implementation status as of execution flagged); data-infrastructure rollback (USDA ERS termination; CDC FoodNet PA-3 disaggregation absence). The three patterns are documented as co-occurring through separate decisions, not inferred as a coordinated program (per XC-9 confabulation guard). The structural consequence is that the SD1 federal architecture's capacity to identify, respond to, and remediate disparate-impact patterns at PA-3 sub-area level operates within a narrowing capacity envelope at exactly the moment when the documented compound-disadvantage geography concentrates the patterns the architecture would address.

**The SNAP benefit-vs-need gap as structural consequence — not an individual-choice failure.** The arithmetic in §6 Pathway 1 shows what the architecture produces at the constituent level. SNAP FY25 maximum benefit ~$8.05/person/day for a household of 4 = $32.20 nominal × corner-store unit-price premium 10-30% [Source 9] = $22.50-$29.30 effective purchasing power in supermarket-pricing terms; plus post-2021 27% food cost inflation [Source 11]. The gap is not produced by the household — it is produced by the intersection of benefit-rate calibration + retail-geography distribution + price-premium pattern at the pathway intersection. PDPH Healthy Corner Store Initiative + Food Bucks operate structurally against the gap at the local layer; their coverage by sub-area is a deepening target. Cross-ref SD3 (pharmacy-as-multi-product access infrastructure post-Rite-Aid pharmacy desert produces overlapping pharmacy-and-grocery access geography in the same compound-disadvantage sub-areas).

**The independent / small organization burden differential — XC-11 application at SD1 layer.** The first-pass focus on conglomerated-brand cosmetics manufacturers under MoCRA, on chain-supermarket SNAP-authorized retailers, and on the federal regulatory architecture in the abstract underweighted the differential reach of these regulatory architectures to small / Black-owned cosmetics manufacturers, to independent corner stores, to small dietary supplement retailers, and to small carnicerías and small ethnic-market retail in South / Southwest Philadelphia and along West Philadelphia 52nd Street corridor and Mt. Airy. MoCRA's small-business exemption thresholds (facility-registration + product-listing thresholds at small-business levels) [D — MoCRA statutory thresholds] potentially exempt the small / Black-owned cosmetics manufacturers most plausibly serving PA-3 Black women cosmetics consumers from the safety-substantiation regime that MoCRA established to address documented disparate-impact patterns. **The small-business exemption may, in operational consequence, exclude from the remediation regime the precise subset of manufacturers whose products are most concentrated in the consumer-base most affected by the pattern MoCRA was designed to address.** This is a structural-derivation finding [SD; premise: MoCRA exemption thresholds + market-segmentation pattern] requiring verification at MoCRA implementation-detail layer.

---

## §8. Pathway tracing — specific breakdown points

**Pathway 1 breakdown point** [D]: SNAP redemption arithmetic at corner-store retailer concentration produces benefit-vs-need gap at the constituent level. Structural cause: benefit-rate calibration (federal Thrifty Food Plan baseline) + retail-geography distribution (PDPH Healthy Corner Store coverage limited; supermarket access geography variable) + price-premium pattern (corner-store unit-price premium 10-30%).

**Pathway 2 breakdown point** [D for implementation-lag; SI for chemical-exposure pathway pending peer-reviewed retrieval]: MoCRA implementation lag through 2026-2028 leaves Black PA-3 women cosmetics consumers in disparate-exposure window. Structural cause: phased final-regulation timeline + small-business exemption threshold reach + market-segmentation by demographic.

**Pathway 3 breakdown point** [D]: PDPH OFP routine inspection density variation by sub-area [SD] — direct retrieval target F4-T2P2-SD1-2. Structural cause: PDPH OFP staffing distribution + complaint-driven inspection volume + Healthy Corner Store Initiative geographic coverage.

**Pathway 4 breakdown point** [D]: DSHEA's structurally light pre-market regime + FDA enforcement post-marketing only. Structural cause: 1994 statutory framework predates contemporary supplement-market expansion; FDA enforcement priority allocation under capacity-erosion pressure.

---

## §9. Representation Question

**Element 1 — What the legal framework formally provides.** SD1 federal-state-local architecture provides comprehensive nominal coverage: FFDCA Chapter IV product safety; FSMA preventive controls; DSHEA supplement regime; FALCPA-FASTER allergen labeling; MoCRA cosmetics modernization; SNAP / WIC / NSLP nutrition-assistance Spending Clause flow; PA Food Safety Act + PADA + Title 6 Chapter 6-300 + PDPH OFP retail-level enforcement; PDPH Get Healthy Philly + Healthy Corner Store + Food Bucks structural-actor remediation infrastructure.

**Element 2 — What PA-3 constituents actually receive.** 21.2% regional FI; 28%+ Black PA-3 FI; 36.9% household FI in studied N. Phila tracts; SNAP benefit-vs-need gap (FY25 max ~$8.05/person/day vs. corner-store unit-price premium 10-30% vs. post-2021 27% food cost inflation); MoCRA implementation lag through 2026-2028 leaves Black PA-3 women cosmetics consumers in disparate-exposure window; sub-area inspection density variation [SD].

**Element 3 — What accounts for the gap.** Three structurally co-occurring patterns: (a) federal capacity erosion (April 2025 FDA RIF; HFP/OII reorganization; April 2025 USDA workforce reductions); (b) data-infrastructure rollback (USDA ERS termination; CDC FoodNet PA-3 disaggregation absence) — what the federal government can no longer see, the architecture can no longer respond to; (c) MoCRA implementation lag interacting with small-business exemption threshold reach (XC-11 — small / Black-owned cosmetics manufacturers may operate outside the safety-substantiation regime that addresses the documented pattern).

**Element 4 — What the gap means for the representation analysis.** SD1's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt. The gap is not a function of program eligibility — eligible PA-3 constituents broadly enroll. The gap is a function of (a) what the formal architecture *does not* address (corner-store retail geography; SNAP benefit-vs-need gap at the calibration layer; MoCRA exemption-threshold reach to small / Black-owned cosmetics manufacturers); (b) what the formal architecture *does not yet* address through implementation lag (MoCRA 2026-2028 phased implementation); (c) what the data infrastructure *characterizes less and less well* (USDA ERS termination). The representation gap in SD1 falls sharpest on Black PA-3 households in N./Northwest Philadelphia Core and on Black PA-3 women cosmetics consumers across all four sub-areas — the populations the federal regulatory tools would have most substantively benefited and the populations most affected by tool implementation-lag, capacity erosion, and data-infrastructure rollback.

