  Independent / small organization layer (XC-11)
    → independent halal / kosher butchers (West Phila 52nd St; Mt. Airy)
    → small carnicerías (S. Phila Italian Market + Vietnamese + Mexican)
    → USDA-exempt retail under Title 6 § 6-301

PA-3 CONSTITUENT LEVEL (aggregate categories per Standard 13)
  SNAP-using households at ~130% FPL purchasing cheaper-cut poultry
    → cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway post-Framework withdrawal
  Halal- and kosher-observant consumers across W. Phila + Mt. Airy + S. Phila
    → ritual-slaughter retail-corridor pathway
  PA-3 retail butcher / deli operations with USDA-exempt portion
    → Title 6 § 6-301 retail jurisdictional pathway
```

---

## §3. Supporting prose

The SD2 architecture's structural-vulnerability pattern is qualitatively different from SD1's: federal-direct slaughter and processing inspection capacity at FSIS Philadelphia District 60 concentrates demand because Pennsylvania has elected non-cooperative MPI status (the state does not operate a state Meat and Poultry Inspection program; federal inspection covers all PA-3-relevant federally-inspected establishments) [D]. The structural concentration is not a pattern of choice at the federal level — it is a structural co-occurrence of state-MPI absence + federal capacity erosion + Salmonella regulatory-tool withdrawal [SD; per XC-9 confabulation guard, the three patterns co-occur through separate decisions].

Inline labeling: FMIA / PPIA / EPIA carry HIGH statutory stability (foundational 1906 / 1967 amendments) [D]. FSIS Philadelphia District 60 carries MODERATE-HIGH administrative vulnerability — the April 2025 USDA workforce reductions (11,300+ deferred resignations effective; XC-9 timestamp discipline applied) reduce federal-direct capacity at the moment the PA non-cooperative MPI status concentrates demand on that capacity [D + SD]. PADA carries MODERATE administrative vulnerability — the non-cooperative MPI is a structural-status decision, not capacity-erosion. PDPH OFP at retail interface carries MODERATE administrative vulnerability — local capacity stable.

Anchor institution analysis: FSIS Philadelphia District 60 at Mellon Independence Center is the federal-cluster anchor at SD2 layer; engagement scoring HIGH at the federally-inspected-establishment dimension and reduced post-2025 workforce reductions. Independent / small organization layer (XC-11): independent halal / kosher butchers + small carnicerías + USDA-exempt retail operate within state-PADA + local-PDPH-OFP jurisdiction at retail and outside FSIS direct jurisdiction at retail per Title 6 §6-301 [D]. The XC-11 differential burden operates at PA Food Safety Act + Title 6 §6-300 + Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional interface — small retail operations carry compliance burden differential against the federally-inspected supply chain that feeds them.

---

## §4. Statistical profile

National Salmonella baseline: ~1.35M illnesses/year nationally [D, CDC FoodNet]; ~26,500 hospitalizations; ~420 deaths annually [D]. Withdrawn Salmonella Framework for Raw Poultry Products (90 FR 17344) addressed approximately 25% of foodborne Salmonella illness through preventive-controls overlay [D]; the withdrawal eliminates the regulatory tool addressing this share.

PA-3 specific baseline: F4-T2P2-SD2-1 P1 direct disaggregation pending; structural-derivation [SD] estimate from national rate × PA-3 population × demographic exposure profile yields PA-3-specific Salmonella incidence in the range consistent with national rate and PA-3 demographics. CDC FoodNet PA-3 disaggregation absence (cross-cut SD7) limits direct-evidence retrieval [D].

FSIS-inspected establishments — geographic concentration: federally-inspected slaughter and processing operations within PA-3 limited (most slaughter operations outside city in surrounding counties); processing operations more present; retail-level operations under Title 6 §6-301 carve-out [D].

FSIS workforce affecting PA-3: April 2025 USDA workforce reductions (11,300+ deferred resignations effective) [D, XC-9 timestamp]; FSIS Philadelphia District 60 staffing actuals as of execution flagged F4-T2P2-SD2-2 P2 — pre-2025 baseline ~50 inspectors per District 60 staffing typical baseline [SD; structural inference from FSIS District staffing patterns].

PA-3 meat / poultry retail infrastructure: West Philadelphia 52nd Street corridor halal density (concentration of independent halal butchers serving Muslim PA-3 community) [D]; Mt. Airy kosher concentration (concentration of independent kosher retail serving Jewish PA-3 community) [D]; South Philadelphia Italian Market + Vietnamese / Mexican carnicería + halal corridor (concentration of small / independent ethnic-market retail) [D]. Documented retailer-corridor data at sub-area resolution flagged F4-T2P2-SD2-3 P1.

Cross-domain cross-ref: D2 Salmonella illness chronic-disease cross-cutting [SD]; D9 fiscal flow at SNAP-redemption interface [SD]; D11 workforce-protection at slaughter-processing labor (boundary-adjacent G1-SD2-03) [SD].

---

## §5. Geographic variation across four sub-areas

**North/Northwest Philadelphia Core** [D]: cheaper-cut poultry retail concentration; SNAP-redemption volume highest in PA-3; FI concentration highest [Source 9 cross-cut]; small / independent retail mix variable.

**West Philadelphia Core** [D]: 52nd Street corridor halal-density retail concentration (independent halal butchers serving Muslim PA-3 community); broader cheaper-cut retail mix; Cobbs Creek-area FI concentration cross-cut.

**Northwest Philadelphia (Mt. Airy)** [D]: kosher concentration (independent kosher retail serving Jewish PA-3 community); mixed-income demographic; lower documented FI; supermarket access more available.

**South/Southwest Philadelphia** [D]: Italian Market + Vietnamese / Mexican carnicería + halal corridor concentration (small / independent ethnic-market retail); USDA-exempt retail under Title 6 §6-301 disproportionately concentrated; Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional pathway most operative here.

Sub-area summary: ritual-slaughter / ethnic-market retail concentrates in West Phila + Mt. Airy + S. Phila; cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway concentrates in compound-disadvantage sub-areas (N./Northwest Phila Core + portions of W. Phila Core + S. Phila) [SD; premise: SNAP redemption volume + retail mix]. Cross-sub-area observation: independent / small retail (XC-11) carries the documented retailer-corridor concentration; corporate-chain supermarket retail is structurally different in regulatory pathway.

---

## §6. Aggregate Pathway Analysis

**Pathway 1 — SNAP-using PA-3 household at 130% FPL purchasing cheaper-cut poultry as primary protein** [D + SD pathway].

Triggering event: monthly SNAP redemption with cheaper-cut poultry as protein selection. Institutional steps: federally-inspected slaughter at FSIS-inspected facility; cold-chain distribution; retail at SNAP-authorized retailer (possibly within Title 6 §6-301 retail layer); home preparation. Decision point: Pathogen Reduction/HACCP Final Rule operative at slaughter; the **withdrawn Salmonella Framework** would have added preventive-controls overlay addressing ~25% of foodborne Salmonella illness — withdrawal April 25, 2025 (90 FR 17344) eliminates that tool [D, XC-9 timestamp].

Documented breakdown patterns: cheaper-cut concentration (parts vs. whole-bird selection by household economics) intersects Salmonella prevalence patterns at slaughter — cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway unaddressed by general SD2 regulatory tools post-Framework-withdrawal; PA non-cooperative MPI concentrates federal-FSIS demand on reduced-capacity FSIS Philadelphia District 60 simultaneously [D + SD].

**XC-10 ITD note appended**: the Salmonella Framework was proposed 2024 → withdrawn April 25, 2025 → January 2026 FSIS public meeting on regulatory pathway — felt-reality reach for SNAP-using PA-3 households purchasing cheaper-cut poultry remains at pre-Framework baseline; the implementation-time-delay between framework-withdrawal and replacement-pathway-availability is the structural gap.

**Pathway 2 — Halal-observant consumer in West Philadelphia 52nd Street corridor / kosher-observant consumer in Mt. Airy seeking ritually-slaughtered meat** [D pathway].

Triggering event: religious-observance-driven purchase of ritually-slaughtered meat at retail corridor. Institutional steps: FMIA § 1902 ritual-slaughter exemption operates at slaughter; retail-level certification varies by retailer (third-party ritual-certifying organizations operate in halal and kosher markets); independent halal butcher (West Phila 52nd Street corridor) or independent kosher retailer (Mt. Airy) functions as retail anchor [D].

Documented breakdown patterns: independent halal / kosher retail concentration in specific corridors functions structurally — accommodation capacity is partly geographic, partly institutional. Documented retailer-corridor data at sub-area level pending F4-T2P2-SD2-3 P1.

**XC-11 application — small / independent retail differential**: independent halal butchers and independent kosher retailers operate as small / independent organizations within the PA Food Safety Act + Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional layer; they function within state-PADA + local-PDPH-OFP retail jurisdiction and outside FSIS direct jurisdiction at retail. The compliance burden + customer-trust + Salmonella-tool-withdrawal effects fall differentially: small retail operations rely on the upstream federally-inspected supply chain (whose tool just withdrew) without direct FSIS-jurisdiction recourse at retail.

**Pathway 3 — PA-3 retail butcher / deli operation with USDA-exempt portion (small carnicería / ethnic-market retail)** [D pathway].

Triggering event: opening or operating retail butcher / deli with USDA-exempt portion under Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional carve-out. Institutional steps: PA Retail Food Facility Safety Act + PADA retail inspection at exempt portion; PDPH OFP at retail food licensing layer (Title 6 Chapter 6-300 + §6-301 boundary); compliance with state + local food code [D].

**XC-11 application**: small carnicerías + small ethnic-market retail in S. Phila Italian Market + Vietnamese / Mexican corridors + halal-corridor retail across sub-areas operate concentrated in this pathway; the compliance burden under PA Retail Food Facility Safety Act + Title 6 §6-300 + §6-301 jurisdictional layering operates differentially against corporate-chain butcher / deli operations whose compliance infrastructure absorbs regulatory burden differently.

---

## §7. Conversational Narrative Note

**What the SD2 architecture does to PA-3 residents in stated categories — central insight.** PA's non-cooperative MPI status concentrates federal-FSIS demand on FSIS Philadelphia District 60 at the precise moment the federal capacity at District 60 is reduced through the April 2025 USDA workforce reductions (11,300+ deferred resignations effective; XC-9 timestamp). The structural concentration is not a pattern of choice — it is a structural co-occurrence of state-level MPI absence + federal capacity erosion + Salmonella regulatory-tool withdrawal at the cheaper-cut consumer pathway level. The inspection regime that nominally protects all PA-3 meat consumers concentrates demand on a reduced-capacity federal apparatus while withdrawing the tool that would have addressed the largest single foodborne Salmonella source.

**The MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence at SD2 layer.** Three documented patterns co-occur (per XC-9 confabulation guard — co-occurrence not coordinated program): rule withdrawal (April 25, 2025 Salmonella Framework for Raw Poultry Products withdrawn; 90 FR 17344); federal capacity erosion (April 2025 USDA workforce reductions; FSIS District 60 staffing actuals flagged); state-level non-cooperation pattern (PA's structurally-determined non-cooperative MPI status remains operative). The simultaneity at SD2 produces structural elevation of foodborne-illness exposure that the federal architecture would have most pointedly addressed. Critical XC-9 distinction: the *withdrawal* is documented [D] — the inferred-coherence framing that would link the withdrawal to a coordinated MAHA program is not made; the documented co-occurrence framing is made. January 2026 FSIS public meeting on Salmonella regulatory pathway represents potential replacement-tool development; status as of execution flagged.

**The XC-11 differential at SD2: ritual-slaughter retail + small / independent ethnic-market retail + USDA-exempt retail concentration.** Independent halal / kosher butchers (West Phila 52nd St; Mt. Airy); small carnicerías (S. Phila); USDA-exempt retail (Title 6 §6-301) — the compliance burden + customer-trust + Salmonella-tool-withdrawal effects fall on small / independent retail differentially against corporate-chain supermarket retail. The small / independent retail anchors specific PA-3 communities in specific sub-areas — losing the regulatory architecture's full operative reach at slaughter (Salmonella Framework withdrawal) means the small / independent retail end of the supply chain absorbs more of the structural risk.

**Key human consequence — Pathway 1 SNAP-using cheaper-cut consumers.** For SNAP-using PA-3 households purchasing cheaper-cut poultry as primary protein in compound-disadvantage sub-areas — North/Northwest Philadelphia Core; portions of West Philadelphia Core; South/Southwest Philadelphia — the simultaneity of withdrawal + capacity erosion + state-non-cooperation produces a structural elevation of foodborne-illness exposure that the federal architecture (specifically the proposed Salmonella Framework) had developed and withdrew. Cross-ref D2 chronic-disease cross-cutting; SD3 pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid (Salmonella treatment pathway access affected if pharmacy access compromised; cross-sub-domain access architecture).

---

## §8. Pathway tracing — specific breakdown points

**Pathway 1 breakdown point** [D]: cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway unaddressed by general SD2 tools post-Framework withdrawal. Structural cause: Pathogen Reduction/HACCP 1996 framework operative; Salmonella Framework would have layered preventive-controls + cheaper-cut-specific addressing; withdrawal removes the cheaper-cut-specific layer.

**Pathway 2 breakdown point** [D + SD]: ritual-slaughter retail-corridor capacity is partly geographic, partly institutional [SD; premise: documented retailer-corridor data sub-area concentration]. F4-T2P2-SD2-3 P1 retrieval target.

**Pathway 3 breakdown point** [D]: Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional carve-out at retail produces split-jurisdiction compliance burden for small / independent ethnic-market retail. Structural cause: federal-FSIS jurisdiction ends at retail; state-PADA + local-PDPH-OFP picks up; compliance-burden distribution falls differentially on small operations.

---

## §9. Representation Question

**Element 1 — What the legal framework formally provides.** SD2 federal-state-local architecture provides comprehensive nominal coverage: FMIA / PPIA / EPIA mandatory inspection at slaughter; Pathogen Reduction/HACCP preventive controls (1996); FMIA §1902 ritual-slaughter exemption; PA Food Safety Act + PA Retail Food Facility Safety Act; Title 6 §6-301 retail jurisdictional structure; PADA retail inspection oversight; PDPH OFP retail-level enforcement.

**Element 2 — What PA-3 constituents actually receive.** Cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway unaddressed by general SD2 regulatory tools post-Framework withdrawal; PA non-cooperative MPI status concentrates federal-FSIS demand on FSIS Philadelphia District 60 simultaneously with April 2025 USDA workforce reductions; halal / kosher accommodation operates through independent retailer corridors at sub-area level; small carnicerías and small ethnic-market retail concentrated in specific sub-areas operate within Title 6 §6-301 jurisdictional pathway.

**Element 3 — What accounts for the gap.** Three structurally co-occurring patterns at SD2: (a) Salmonella Framework withdrawal April 25, 2025 (90 FR 17344) eliminates the regulatory tool addressing ~25% of foodborne Salmonella illness, including cheaper-cut exposure pathway; (b) April 2025 USDA workforce reductions (11,300+ deferred resignations effective) erode federal-direct capacity at FSIS District 60; (c) PA non-cooperative MPI status structurally concentrates federal-FSIS demand on the reduced-capacity federal apparatus. The XC-11 differential burden falls on small / independent retail anchoring specific PA-3 communities; the cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway concentrates on lower-income predominantly Black PA-3 consumer households.

**Element 4 — What the gap means for the representation analysis.** SD2's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt. The gap is a function of (a) what the formal architecture *withdrew* (Salmonella Framework for Raw Poultry Products); (b) what the formal architecture *cannot deliver at full capacity* (FSIS District 60 reduced capacity post-2025 workforce reductions); (c) what the state architecture *structurally does not provide* (non-cooperative MPI status); (d) what the small / independent retail layer *carries differentially* (XC-11 burden + customer-trust). The representation gap in SD2 falls sharpest on SNAP-using PA-3 households purchasing cheaper-cut poultry in compound-disadvantage sub-areas and on PA-3 communities served by small / independent ritual-slaughter and ethnic-market retail.

