## §6. Aggregate Pathway Analysis

**Pathway 1 — PA-3 Medicare Part D beneficiary on IRA-MFP-list drugs in pharmacy-desert sub-area** [D pathway — defining D4 SD3 paradox].

Triggering event: prescription fill for IRA-MFP-list drug (effective January 1, 2026) at sub-area where pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid operates. Institutional steps: prescriber writes prescription; beneficiary attempts fill at nearest pharmacy; if Rite Aid was the nearest pharmacy and is closed (one of 5 documented PA-3 closures), beneficiary navigates to alternative pharmacy; chain vs. independent mix change post-Rite-Aid affects fill availability; specialty drugs may require specialty pharmacy routing through health-system-affiliated infrastructure.

Illustrative calculation [D rates × stated category]: IRA MFP Year 1 first-round prices on 10 drugs (CMS-published) reduce list price 38-79% [D, CMS]; for a Medicare Part D beneficiary in an SVI-high tract with documented Rite Aid closure, transportation to alternative pharmacy is the felt-reality reach barrier — the federal price reduction is operative at the pricing layer, but the physical access pathway from prescription-write to fill-completion has structurally narrowed. **The defining D4 SD3 paradox**: IRA's pricing protection (effective January 1, 2026) arrives operative simultaneous with PA-3 physical pharmacy access architecture collapse (Rite Aid closures August 22-24, 2025; ~80+ PA pharmacies closed since January 2024; ~20% PA pharmacy reduction; SVI 30-40% closure correlation).

XC-10 ITD note: pricing-protection effective date precedes felt-reality reach for beneficiaries in pharmacy-desert sub-areas; implementation-time-delay between IRA MFP effective date (January 1, 2026) and operational pharmacy access reach is structurally ongoing.

XC-11 application: independent pharmacies could plausibly fill some Rite Aid gap if PBM reimbursement architecture supported viability; PA Act 77 of 2024 reform addresses this at state layer (staggered implementation).

**Pathway 2 — PA-3 OUD patient accessing buprenorphine via post-X-waiver pathway** [D + SD pathway].

Triggering event: OUD diagnosis + treatment-pursuit at MAT-Act-eligible prescriber. Institutional steps: MAT Act 2022 eliminated DATA 2000 X-waiver requirement (~3,000-4,000 PA-3 prescriber-eligible cadre [SD]); prescription written; pharmacy fill subject to suspicious-order monitoring constraint; methadone vs. buprenorphine racial-pattern asymmetry documented in academic literature (cross-ref SD6).

Documented breakdown patterns: suspicious-order monitoring constrains pharmacy availability differentially by community pharmacy type and location (cross-ref G1-SD6-04 MAT Act / pharmacy availability gap); pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid intersects this pathway directly. Cross-ref D2 chronic disease burden + pharmacy access intersection.

**Pathway 3 — PA-3 resident in pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid (general prescription fill pathway)** [D pathway].

Triggering event: any prescription fill in documented pharmacy-desert tract. Institutional steps: prescription writing; nearest-pharmacy navigation; chain vs. independent mix change post-Rite-Aid; transportation pattern to nearest available pharmacy. Documented breakdown: SVI 30-40% closure correlation [Source 28]; sub-area variation by closure concentration.

XC-11 application — independent pharmacies as cross-cutting case. PBM reimbursement architecture as structural cause of independent pharmacy attrition (G1-SD3-03); PA Act 77 of 2024 PBM reform addresses at state layer with staggered implementation; pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid concentrates impact differentially on independents.

**Pathway 4 — PA-3 chronic-condition patient requiring biologic via specialty pharmacy through health-system-affiliated infrastructure** [D + SD pathway].

Triggering event: chronic-condition diagnosis requiring biologic with specialty pharmacy routing. Institutional steps: prescriber writes prescription; specialty-pharmacy routing through Penn / CHOP / Jefferson / Temple specialty-pharmacy infrastructure; biologic dispensing volume concentrated at health-system-affiliated specialty pharmacies. Documented breakdown: G1-SD3-08 specialty pharmacy access asymmetry by health system affiliation; D9 IRA fiscal flow specificity at biologic-pricing interface; D11 cross-ref clinical trial diversity affecting biologic-population evidence base.

XC-11 application: independent pharmacies functionally excluded from specialty-pharmacy biologic-dispensing pathway; small clinical practices vs. major health systems carry differential specialty-pharmacy referral pathway access.

---

## §7. Conversational Narrative Note

**What the SD3 architecture does to PA-3 residents in stated categories — central insight.** IRA Medicare Drug Price Negotiation Program first-round Maximum Fair Prices effective January 1, 2026 arrives operative at exactly the moment when PA-3 physical pharmacy access architecture has collapsed through ~20% PA pharmacy reduction. The intersection is structurally derivable from documented co-occurrence: federal pricing protection + state pharmacy attrition + sub-area-disparate Rite Aid closure pattern (SVI 30-40% closure correlation; documented PA-3 closures concentrated in S./SW Phila and N./Northwest Phila Core). The protection cannot reach the PA-3 constituent who cannot physically reach a pharmacy. **The pricing-protection-vs-access-collapse paradox is the central human consequence at SD3, not the regulatory architecture in the abstract.**

**The MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence at SD3 layer + XC-9 timestamp discipline.** Three documented patterns co-occur (per XC-9 confabulation guard — co-occurrence not coordinated program): federal capacity erosion (April 2025 FDA RIF ~3,500; ~20% workforce loss through early 2026; CDER 5-director sequence in 2025); state-level pharmacy infrastructure attrition (~20% PA pharmacy reduction; Rite Aid PA-wide closures by August 22, 2025); operational-redirection pressure on the federal pricing-protection tool (Trump 2 EO 14273 IRA MFP implementation guidance status flagged F4-T2P2-SD3-3 P1; XC-9 timestamp). The structural consequence at SD3 is qualitatively different from SD1 or SD2: the federal architecture has *implemented* a substantive pricing-protection tool (IRA MFP effective January 1, 2026; this is documented [D] policy implementation), simultaneous with documented physical access collapse. Implementation, capacity erosion, and access collapse co-occur — the implemented protection cannot reach the constituent in the access-collapsed sub-area.

**The CVM sub-section deepening — XC-11 cross-cut.** First-pass CVM treatment was thinner than human-drug components per WL6 substructure decision (G1-SD3-07). Second-pass deepening: CVM operates federal-direct from Rockville with no Philadelphia office; PA Veterinary Medical Board licenses ~12,000 PA veterinarians [SD]; companion animal drug access pathway interfaces with retail pharmacy infrastructure (independent veterinary practices vs. corporate veterinary chains operate within similar PBM-like reimbursement structural pressures); aquaculture/livestock interface with FSIS animal drug residue coordination has been affected by the April 2025 USDA RIF (cross-ref SD2; this is an important structural cross-cut — federal capacity erosion at USDA-FSIS affects FSIS-CVM coordination operationally, even though both agencies' statutory architectures remain stable).

**The XC-11 differential at SD3: independent pharmacies as the load-bearing case.** PBM reimbursement architecture as structural cause of independent pharmacy attrition (G1-SD3-03) — independents face MAC pricing pressure + DIR fees + reimbursement-rate compression that chain pharmacies absorb differentially through scale. PA Act 77 of 2024 PBM reform addresses this at state layer with staggered implementation; the implementation interval is itself a felt-reality gap (XC-10 ITD note — when does PA Act 77 reform reach the independent pharmacy operational viability layer?). Pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid intersects directly: independents could plausibly fill some Rite Aid gap *if* the reimbursement architecture supported small-pharmacy viability; the structural-architecture interaction is what makes independents the load-bearing XC-11 case at SD3. Specialty pharmacy access asymmetry by health-system affiliation (G1-SD3-08) compounds this — independents are functionally excluded from the specialty-pharmacy biologic-dispensing pathway, concentrating biologic-access at major health-system specialty pharmacies (Penn / CHOP / Jefferson / Temple). **The constituent-level consequence**: PA-3 patients requiring specialty biologics through small clinical practices not affiliated with major health systems navigate a fundamentally different access architecture than patients with major health system affiliation.

