## §9. Representation Question

**Element 1 — Formal provision.** SD3 federal-state-local architecture: FFDCA Chapter V; PHSA §351; Hatch-Waxman; BPCIA; user-fee architecture (PDUFA / GDUFA / BsUFA / OMUFA); IRA MFP (effective January 1, 2026; 10 drugs first round); CSA at SD3 interface; MAT Act 2022; PA Act 77 of 2024 PBM reform; PA Pharmacy Act + PA Drug Act; ABC-MAP. CMS Region III Philadelphia administering Medicare Part D + IRA MFP.

**Element 2 — Actual receipt.** IRA MFP pricing protection effective + ~20% PA pharmacy reduction simultaneous; PA-3 sub-area Rite Aid closures concentrated in S./SW Phila + N./Northwest Phila Core; SVI 30-40% closure correlation; specialty pharmacy access asymmetry by health-system affiliation; ~3,000-4,000 PA-3 buprenorphine prescriber-eligible cadre [SD] under suspicious-order monitoring constraint; CVM sub-section operational reach affected by federal-direct-only architecture + FSIS coordination capacity erosion.

**Element 3 — Account for the gap.** Three structurally co-occurring patterns at SD3: (a) federal capacity erosion (April 2025 FDA RIF; CDER 5-director sequence in 2025); (b) state-level pharmacy infrastructure attrition (~20% PA pharmacy reduction; Rite Aid closures); (c) operational-redirection pressure on federal pricing-protection tool (Trump 2 EO 14273 IRA MFP redirection; XC-9 timestamp). XC-11 differential burden: independent pharmacies face PBM reimbursement architecture pressure + scale-dependent reimbursement compression + functional exclusion from specialty pharmacy pathway.

**Element 4 — Meaning for representation analysis.** SD3's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt. The gap is a function of (a) pricing-protection-vs-access-collapse simultaneity (IRA MFP effective + pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid); (b) PBM reimbursement architecture structurally affecting independent pharmacy viability (PA Act 77 of 2024 staggered implementation pending); (c) specialty pharmacy access asymmetry by health-system affiliation; (d) federal capacity erosion compounding review-and-enforcement capacity. The representation gap in SD3 falls sharpest on Medicare Part D beneficiaries on IRA-MFP-list drugs in pharmacy-desert sub-areas, on PA-3 OUD patients accessing buprenorphine in pharmacy-desert sub-areas (cross-ref SD6), and on PA-3 chronic-condition patients requiring biologic without major-health-system affiliation. **The defining D4 SD3 representation paradox**: the federal pricing tool that the architecture deployed to substantively address Medicare Part D affordability arrives at PA-3 simultaneous with physical access collapse — the protection landed in a context that structurally diminishes its operational reach.

