## §9. Representation Question

**Element 1 — Formal provision.** SD4 federal-state-local architecture: PHSA §351 + BPCIA biologics; FFDCA §513-§520 devices; ACIP authority + Vaccines for Children; CMS Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model; PA Drug Act + PADoH school immunization; PDPH Division of Disease Control. Casgevy + Lyfgenia approved December 2023; cell/gene therapy ATC infrastructure at Penn + CHOP.

**Element 2 — Actual receipt.** Cell/gene therapy capacity geographically present at University City; uptake at Penn / CHOP pending quantification with documented low pattern [SD]; vaccine policy implementation status "in flux" with prior schedule operative pending March 2026 federal court block; algorithmic clinical decision-support tools operating under CLIA-only oversight without federal premarket validation post-LDT vacatur; small-practice burden differential at vaccine administration + LDT-replacement.

**Element 3 — Account for the gap.** Three structurally co-occurring patterns at SD4 (most volatile federal layer in D4): (a) rule-availability erosion (LDT vacatur March 31, 2025); (b) federal capacity erosion (CBER + CDRH leadership uncertainty + April 2025 FDA RIF); (c) institutional / litigation disruption (ACIP overhaul + vaccine policy litigation flux). The Philadelphia paradox upstream causes: cost + ATC capacity + multi-month treatment cycle + clinical trial diversity gap legacy. XC-11 differential at small clinical practices + small specialty practices.

**Element 4 — Meaning for representation analysis.** SD4's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt, sharpest at the cell/gene therapy access layer where geographic proximity + therapy approval + structural barriers operate simultaneously. The gap is a function of (a) Philadelphia paradox structural barriers (cost + ATC capacity + clinical trial diversity gap legacy); (b) vaccine policy implementation flux during litigation period; (c) LDT vacatur leaving algorithmic clinical decision-support without federal premarket validation; (d) federal capacity erosion at CBER + CDRH + ACIP. The representation gap in SD4 falls sharpest on Black PA-3 SCD patients in sub-areas with documented elevated SCD prevalence (N./Northwest Philadelphia Core; Cobbs Creek-area West Philadelphia Core; S. Phila Black portions) — the population the federal regulatory architecture approved Casgevy and Lyfgenia for and the population for whom geographic proximity to therapy capacity coexists with structural exclusion from operational access. **The defining D4 representation paradox** (G1-SD4-01 = G1-XC-05): the architecture that approved the therapy operates in proximity to the population most affected; access remains structurally constrained.

