## §6. Aggregate Pathway Analysis

**Pathway 1 — Black PA-3 menthol smoker in compound-disadvantage sub-area (defining SD5 paradox)** [D pathway].

Triggering event: ongoing menthol cigarette use by Black PA-3 smoker in N./Northwest Phila Core or portions of W. Phila Core or S. Phila. Institutional steps: federal regulatory architecture under TCA 2009 + PMTA framework + flavor regulation authority operative; **menthol withdrawal RIN 0910-AI60 January 2025** eliminates federal regulatory tool addressing the documented disparate-impact pattern [D, XC-9 timestamp]; PA-state architecture (PA Act 14 Tobacco 21; PA CIAA) operative at age + indoor-air dimensions; local architecture (Title 6 §6-700; Title 10 §10-700) operative at retail density + buffer + licensing dimensions.

Documented breakdown patterns: ~85% of Black smokers use menthol vs. ~30% of white smokers [D]; menthol cigarettes documented as primary contributor to Black smoking-related mortality differential [D]; menthol withdrawal preamble explicitly cited disparate-impact data [D — preamble retrieval F4-T2P2-SD5-2 P1]. **The defining D4 SD5 paradox**: the federal regulatory architecture had developed and was about to deploy a substantive disparate-impact remediation tool (menthol cigarette ban); the tool was withdrawn in January 2025 simultaneous with the April 2025 CTP Office of Regulations RIF that decimated regulatory-development capacity. The withdrawal occurred at the moment when remediation was federally available and capacity-supported; the rescission + capacity decimation co-occurrence produces structural foreclosure of the disparate-impact remediation pathway through the architecture's own regulatory channels.

XC-11 application: small tobacco retailers under density cap + 500-foot school buffer + license fee architecture carry differential compliance burden; documented SNAP-authorized retailer interior advertising 3.43x pattern operates concentrated at small retailers; the marketing-density pattern at small-retailer layer compounds the disparate-impact pattern through marketing exposure differential.

**Pathway 2 — PA-3 underage resident (under 21) seeking tobacco / vaping products** [D pathway].

Triggering event: under-21 individual seeking tobacco / vaping product purchase. Institutional steps: federal Tobacco 21 (FFDCA Chapter IX as amended 2019); PA Act 14 of 2020 (PA Tobacco 21 effective July 1, 2020); Title 6 §6-700 retailer compliance architecture; Title 10 §10-700 administrative architecture; PDPH retail enforcement; federal user-fee-funded enforcement at retail through CTP partnership with state/local.

Documented breakdown patterns: retailer compliance variation by sub-area; cessation-pharmacotherapy access vector reduction post-pharmacy-desert (cross-ref SD3); under-21 vaping pathway access concentrates at retailer non-compliance points.

**Pathway 3 — Small PA-3 tobacco retailer under density cap + 500-foot school buffer** [D pathway].

Triggering event: opening or operating retail tobacco operation in PA-3 sub-area. Institutional steps: PDPH retail tobacco license application; density-cap compliance check (1-per-1,000 retailer-population ratio); 500-foot school buffer compliance; license fee; ongoing compliance under Title 6 §6-700 + Title 10 §10-700; federal CTP retail compliance enforcement coordination.

XC-11 application: small / independent tobacco retailers carry differential compliance burden against corporate-chain retailers whose compliance infrastructure absorbs regulatory burden differently; the density-cap compliance pressures small retailers asymmetrically while leaving corporate-chain market share more stable. Documented marketing density pattern (SNAP-authorized retailer interior advertising 3.43x) operates concentrated at small retailers in compound-disadvantage sub-areas — small retailers absorb the marketing-density disparate-impact concentration without commensurate access to the upstream corporate-marketing-decision layer where the pattern is structurally produced [SD].

---

## §7. Conversational Narrative Note

**What the SD5 architecture does to PA-3 residents in stated categories — central insight: foreclosed remediation.** The federal SD5 architecture had developed, through the menthol cigarette ban proposed rulemaking, a substantive disparate-impact remediation tool addressing the documented pattern that ~85% of Black smokers use menthol vs. ~30% of white smokers and that menthol cigarettes contribute to Black smoking-related mortality differential. The proposed rulemaking's preamble explicitly cited disparate-impact data; the architecture's own regulatory pathway acknowledged the pattern and developed the remediation tool. **In January 2025, the architecture withdrew the tool.** In April 2025, the architecture decimated its own regulatory-development capacity through the CTP Office of Regulations RIF — "the entire office responsible for drafting new tobacco regulations" [D]. **The disparate-impact remediation pathway through CTP's regulatory channels is structurally foreclosed.** The pattern persists at the constituent level; the federal regulatory tool that would have substantively addressed it withdrew at the moment of remediation availability; the federal capacity to develop a replacement tool has been decimated.

**The MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence at SD5 layer + XC-9 timestamp discipline.** Three documented patterns co-occur at SD5 (per XC-9 confabulation guard): rule withdrawal (menthol cigarette ban RIN 0910-AI60 withdrawn January 2025; characterizing-flavors-in-cigars rule withdrawn); federal capacity decimation (CTP Office of Regulations April 2025 RIF — "entire office responsible for drafting new tobacco regulations" decimated; XC-9 timestamp); industry-favorable substantive direction at moment of disparate-impact remediation availability — the *combination* of withdrawal + capacity decimation forecloses the remediation pathway through CTP's regulatory channels. Critical XC-9 distinction: the *withdrawals* and the *RIF* are documented events with explicit dates and regulatory citations; the inferred-coherence framing that links them to a coordinated MAHA tobacco program is *not* made — the documented co-occurrence framing is made. **SD5 is the defining D4 sub-domain for the industry-favorable-direction-at-disparate-impact-remediation-availability moment** (G1-SD5-01 = G1-XC-08 cross-cut).

**The XC-11 differential at SD5: marketing density at small retailers.** The documented SNAP-authorized retailer interior advertising 3.43x pattern operates concentrated at small / independent retailers in compound-disadvantage sub-areas. The pattern is consequential because the marketing-density disparate-impact concentration is produced upstream at the corporate marketing decision layer (which retailer to advertise in; which advertising material to place; which products to promote) — yet the pattern's enforcement-burden lands at the small-retailer layer through density-cap + buffer + license fee + revocation architecture. Small retailers absorb the marketing-density disparate-impact concentration's compliance-burden differential without commensurate access to the upstream corporate-marketing-decision layer where the pattern is structurally produced. Cross-ref SD1 corner-store unit-price premium architecture; cross-ref SD3 PBM reimbursement architecture as similar XC-11 structural pattern at differential layer.

**Key human consequence — Pathway 1 Black PA-3 menthol smokers in compound-disadvantage sub-areas.** For Black PA-3 menthol smokers in N./Northwest Philadelphia Core + portions of W. Phila Core + S. Phila — sub-areas with documented elevated marketing density (SNAP-authorized retailer interior advertising 3.43x pattern) and documented menthol-smoking-prevalence concentration — the architecture withdrew the federal regulatory tool that would have substantively addressed the disparate-impact pattern at the federal layer, and decimated the regulatory-development capacity that would develop a replacement tool. State and local layers (PA Tobacco 21; PA CIAA; Title 6 §6-700; Title 10 §10-700) operate at age + indoor-air + density + buffer dimensions but cannot federally regulate menthol product itself. The disparate-impact remediation pathway at the federal layer is structurally foreclosed through CTP's regulatory channels. Cross-ref D2 chronic disease + smoking-related mortality intersection; SD3 pharmacy desert reducing cessation-pharmacotherapy access vector (G1-SD5-05).

---

## §8. Pathway tracing — specific breakdown points

**Pathway 1 breakdown point** [D]: menthol withdrawal RIN 0910-AI60 January 2025 + CTP Office of Regulations April 2025 RIF = federal regulatory remediation pathway through CTP channels structurally foreclosed. Structural cause: rule withdrawal + capacity decimation co-occurrence; preamble disparate-impact citation [F4-T2P2-SD5-2 P1] documents what the architecture itself acknowledged.

**Pathway 2 breakdown point** [D]: retailer compliance variation by sub-area + cessation-pharmacotherapy access vector reduction post-pharmacy-desert. Structural cause: federal-state-local enforcement architecture fragmentation + cross-cut SD3.

**Pathway 3 breakdown point** [D + SD]: small-retailer differential compliance burden + marketing-density disparate-impact concentration at small-retailer layer. Structural cause: density-cap enforcement architecture lands at small retailers; corporate-marketing-decision upstream layer not reached by enforcement architecture.

---

## §9. Representation Question

**Element 1 — Formal provision.** SD5 federal-state-local architecture: TCA 2009 + PMTA framework + flavor regulation authority + CTP regulatory development capacity (pre-RIF); PA Tobacco 21; PA Clean Indoor Air Act; Title 6 §6-700 + Title 10 §10-700 retail density + buffer + licensing architecture; PDPH tobacco enforcement.

**Element 2 — Actual receipt.** Menthol cigarette regulatory remediation pathway through CTP channels structurally foreclosed (withdrawal + capacity decimation); marketing-density disparate-impact concentration at small-retailer layer in compound-disadvantage sub-areas; cessation-pharmacotherapy access vector reduction post-pharmacy-desert (cross-ref SD3); state-local age + density + buffer architecture operative at non-menthol-product dimensions.

**Element 3 — Account for the gap.** Three structurally co-occurring patterns at SD5: (a) menthol withdrawal RIN 0910-AI60 January 2025; (b) characterizing-flavors-in-cigars rule withdrawal; (c) CTP Office of Regulations April 2025 RIF decimating regulatory-development capacity. The combination forecloses the federal disparate-impact remediation pathway through CTP's regulatory channels at the moment of remediation availability. XC-11 differential at small-retailer marketing-density concentration; cross-cut SD3 cessation pharmacotherapy access pathway.

**Element 4 — Meaning for representation analysis.** SD5's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt, sharpest at the menthol-product disparate-impact pathway where the federal regulatory architecture acknowledged the pattern (preamble disparate-impact citation), developed the tool (proposed rulemaking), and withdrew the tool (January 2025) at the moment of remediation availability — simultaneous with capacity decimation (April 2025). The gap is a function of (a) federal regulatory tool foreclosure (menthol withdrawal); (b) federal regulatory capacity foreclosure (CTP Office of Regulations RIF); (c) marketing-density disparate-impact concentration at small-retailer layer in compound-disadvantage sub-areas; (d) cessation-pharmacotherapy access vector reduction post-pharmacy-desert. The representation gap in SD5 falls sharpest on Black PA-3 menthol smokers in N./Northwest Philadelphia Core + portions of W. Phila Core + S. Phila — the population the federal regulatory architecture itself acknowledged was disparately affected (preamble) and the population whose remediation pathway through CTP channels is now structurally foreclosed. **The defining D4 SD5 paradox**: the architecture acknowledged the disparate-impact pattern in its own regulatory preamble + developed the substantive remediation tool + withdrew the tool + decimated the regulatory-development capacity — the foreclosure operates against the population the architecture itself identified.

