## §3. Supporting prose

The SD6 chain shows two distinct constitutional pathways operating simultaneously: Commerce Clause regulation of controlled substances (CSA + DEA) and Spending Clause federal funding of addiction treatment infrastructure (SAMHSA / HRSA / Ryan White cross-cut D5) [D]. Inline labeling: CSA carries HIGH statutory stability (foundational 1970); MAT Act carries HIGH statutory stability (2022); Ryan Haight carries HIGH statutory stability with operational flexibility through DEA telemedicine extension to December 2026 [D]. PA Medical Marijuana Act 2016 + ABC-MAP carry HIGH state statutory stability [D].

Federal agency layer carries MODERATE administrative vulnerability at DEA (DEA workforce stable relative to FDA; Schedule III implementation 60-day priority window operative); MODERATE-HIGH at SAMHSA (federal funding pressure on Prevention Point + mobile fentanyl treatment units operative); MODERATE at HRSA (cross-ref D5). State layer at PA DoH Office of Medical Marijuana operates under MODERATE administrative vulnerability; the today-effective Schedule III creates DEA-state coordination pathway requirement [D].

**Today-effective DEA Schedule III Final Order (FR Doc. 2026-08176; effective April 29, 2026)** [D, XC-9 timestamp at four-layer: announcement; effective today; implementation 60-day priority window; felt-reality reach pending PA-3 dispensary uptake] is the central material change at SD6 within the second-pass cycle. The 60-day priority window operates through June 22, 2026; PA-3 dispensary registration uptake during the window flagged F4-T2P2-SD6-2 P1.

Anchor institution analysis: **DBHIDS at Mayor Parker $100M+ wellness ecosystem** is the primary local-layer anchor at SD6; engagement scoring HIGH at expanded local-layer dimension. Prevention Point as harm-reduction anchor (Source 35 demographics: 62% white / 26% Black / 83% unhoused; Kensington compound-incidence locus) is the secondary anchor. **XC-11 at SD6**: smaller harm-reduction / treatment / mutual-aid organizations vs. the DBHIDS + Prevention Point + Mayor Parker ecosystem operate within similar structural funding-pressure but with less capacity to absorb federal funding disruption [SD; premise: organizational-size-vs-funding-disruption-absorption pattern].

