## §6. Aggregate Pathway Analysis

**Pathway 1 — PA-3 OUD patient accessing treatment via post-X-waiver buprenorphine pathway** [D + SD pathway].

Triggering event: OUD diagnosis + treatment-pursuit at MAT-Act-eligible prescriber. Institutional steps: MAT Act 2022 eliminated DATA 2000 X-waiver requirement; ~3,000-4,000 PA-3 prescriber-eligible cadre [SD]; prescription written; pharmacy fill subject to suspicious-order monitoring constraint; methadone vs. buprenorphine racial-pattern asymmetry documented (cross-cut national pattern). Decision point: pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid (cross-ref SD3 G1-SD6-04) reduces buprenorphine pharmacy availability vector at sub-area level.

Documented breakdown patterns: suspicious-order monitoring constrains pharmacy availability differentially by community pharmacy type and location; pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid intersects this pathway directly (cross-cut SD3 G1-SD3-01); cross-ref D2 chronic disease + pharmacy access intersection.

XC-11 application: smaller treatment programs may have less capacity to absorb federal funding pressure than DBHIDS or larger institutional programs; smaller programs serving specific PA-3 community subsets carry differential continuation-vulnerability.

**Pathway 2 — PA-3 resident at Kensington compound-incidence locus** [D pathway — careful framing per G1-SD6-02].

Triggering event: presence of PA-3 resident at Kensington compound-incidence locus geography (boundary partially intersects N./Northwest Phila Core). Institutional steps: parallel-infrastructure (harm-reduction + emergency response + outreach) engagement at locus; Prevention Point demographics 62% white / 26% Black / 83% unhoused [D, Source 35] document the locus engagement pattern.

**Critical framing per G1-SD6-02 — gap-finding without pathologizing**: Prevention Point demographics represent the parallel-infrastructure engagement pattern at the locus itself, not the broader Black PA-3 OUD population engagement pattern. Black PA-3 OUD patients in PA-3 sub-areas with documented OUD prevalence engage treatment pathways through varied infrastructure: traditional treatment programs, peer-support programs, faith-based programs, family / community networks, and other pathways [SD; F4-T2P2-SD6-1 P1 peer-reviewed literature retrieval target on Black PA-3 OUD-population engagement-pattern]. The gap-finding is at the architecture level (parallel-infrastructure organizational engagement at the locus + treatment-pathway diversity at broader population level + structural-pattern documentation), not at the individual-engagement-pattern pathologizing level.

Documented breakdown patterns: medetomidine 83% supply by March 2025 + BTMPS 25% + xylazine continued co-occurrence produces compound chronic-illness profile [SI; F4-T2P2-SD6-4 P2 retrieval]; **both-true framing**: overdose mortality decline ~24% from 2022 peak + Mayor Parker ecosystem expansion + structurally entrenched compound factors all operate simultaneously.

**Pathway 3 — PA-3 medical marijuana patient seeking certification + dispensary access** [D pathway].

Triggering event: qualifying-condition patient seeking PA medical marijuana certification + access. Institutional steps: certifying physician registration + patient certification under PA Act 16 of 2016; dispensary access at PA-3-located dispensary; today-effective DEA Schedule III Final Order (FR Doc. 2026-08176; effective April 29, 2026) creates new federal-state coordination pathway requirement; 60-day priority window through June 22, 2026 for medical marijuana licensee DEA registration.

Decision point: **first 60-day priority window operative today through June 22, 2026** [D, XC-9 four-layer timestamp]; PA-3 dispensary registration uptake during window flagged F4-T2P2-SD6-2 P1.

XC-10 ITD note: Schedule III legal-layer effective today (April 29, 2026); felt-reality reach to PA-3 medical marijuana patient at dispensary access layer pending dispensary registration completion + insurance / Medicare-Medicaid coverage adaptation pathway operationalization.

XC-11 application: smaller dispensary operators (independent vs. multi-state operators) under PA Act 16 architecture carry differential capacity to navigate dual federal-state regulatory pathway during the 60-day priority window.

