## §9. Representation Question

**Element 1 — Formal provision.** SD6 federal-state-local architecture: CSA 1970 + today-effective DEA Schedule III Final Order; MAT Act 2022; Ryan Haight + DEA telemedicine through December 2026; PA Drug Act + PA Medical Marijuana Act 2016; ABC-MAP; SAMHSA federal addiction-treatment funding architecture; DBHIDS + Prevention Point + Mayor Parker $100M+ wellness ecosystem.

**Element 2 — Actual receipt.** Today-effective Schedule III operative; 60-day priority window through June 22, 2026; pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid affects buprenorphine pharmacy availability + cessation pharmacotherapy access vector (cross-ref SD3 + SD5); structurally entrenched compound factors at Kensington compound-incidence locus persist (medetomidine 83% supply by March 2025; BTMPS 25%; xylazine co-occurrence); both-true overdose mortality decline ~24% + Mayor Parker ecosystem expansion + entrenchment co-occurring; XC-11 smaller-organization differential operative.

**Element 3 — Account for the gap.** Three structurally co-occurring patterns at SD6: (a) federal architecture *expansion* at substance-rescheduling layer (today-effective Schedule III) — qualitatively different from SD1-SD5 withdrawal pattern; (b) federal funding pressure at SAMHSA layer affecting Prevention Point + mobile fentanyl treatment units (XC-11 differential); (c) structurally entrenched compound factors at locus level intersecting pharmacy desert + cessation pathway reduction (cross-cut SD3 + SD5). Careful G1-SD6-02 framing: gap-finding at architecture level + treatment-pathway diversity at broader Black PA-3 OUD population level.

**Element 4 — Meaning for representation analysis.** SD6's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt. The gap is a function of (a) pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid affecting buprenorphine + cessation pharmacotherapy access (cross-cut SD3 + SD5); (b) federal funding pressure on harm-reduction infrastructure with XC-11 differential at smaller organizations; (c) structurally entrenched compound-incidence factors at Kensington locus intersecting PA-3 boundary; (d) today-effective Schedule III creating new federal-state coordination pathway requirement at PA-3 dispensary registration layer. The representation gap in SD6 falls sharpest on PA-3 OUD patients in pharmacy-desert sub-areas (cross-cut SD3) and on PA-3 residents at the Kensington compound-incidence locus boundary intersecting N./Northwest Phila Core. **Both-true framing**: overdose mortality decline + Mayor Parker ecosystem expansion + structurally entrenched compound factors all operate simultaneously; the second pass preserves all three as documented patterns.

