## §3. Supporting prose

The SD7 federal-cluster synthesis shows three layers of analysis: statutory layer (HIGH stability composite — federal statutes themselves are not eroding); agency layer (5 of 6 SDs at HIGH or EXTREME admin vulnerability — composite uniquely concentrated in 2025-2026); rule-availability layer (heterogeneous: 5 of 6 SDs document withdrawal / vacatur / redirection; SD6 documents expansion). The three-layer separation is methodologically critical — collapsing across the three layers produces inferred-coherence framing the documented evidence does not support.

**The statutory architecture is intact.** FFDCA, FSMA, DSHEA, FALCPA-FASTER, MoCRA, FMIA, PPIA, EPIA, PHSA §351, Hatch-Waxman, BPCIA, IRA §11001, MAT Act, CSA, FFDCA §513-§520, TCA 2009 — all foundational federal statutes remain in force. The agency capacity to administer these statutes is what has substantively eroded in 2025-2026 [D]. The rule-availability layer shows heterogeneous direction — withdrawal at SD1-SD5 (different specific rules, different specific dates, different specific pathways) and expansion at SD6 (today-effective Schedule III). Treating these heterogeneous documented facts as a coordinated unitary federal program inferentially overshoots the documentation.

**Three-pattern co-occurrence framing replaces "three-vector federal direction."** Per Step 1 §8 advocacy-language tightening + XC-F, the second pass adopts "three-pattern co-occurrence" framing: Pattern 1 (rule withdrawal / vacatur / redirection); Pattern 2 (federal capacity erosion); Pattern 3 (data-infrastructure rollback). The framing is methodologically more precise: "co-occurrence" is the documented fact; "vector" or "direction" implies coherent program; "three-pattern" preserves the heterogeneity within the co-occurrence (different rules, different specific events, different specific timelines) without imposing inferred-coherence framing.

---

