## §6. Aggregate Pathway Analysis (synthesis-level)

Cross-SD aggregate pathways at synthesis layer:

**Federal-cluster pathway**: PA-3 constituent in stated category interacting simultaneously with multiple SDs through federal-cluster anchor at FDA Philadelphia District (SD1 + SD3 + SD4 + SD5) + FSIS Philadelphia District 60 (SD2) + CTP user-fee operations (SD5) + CMS Region III (SD3 + SD4) + DEA (SD6) — the federal-cluster anchor at Mellon Independence Center is the geographic-coincidence point for SD1 + SD2 + SD3 + SD4 + SD5 federal layers. Federal capacity erosion at this single geographic point produces compound effects across multiple SDs simultaneously.

**Anchor-institution pathway**: PA-3 constituent navigating Penn / CHOP / Jefferson / Temple academic-medical-center infrastructure across SD3 specialty pharmacy + SD4 cell/gene therapy + SD6 addiction-treatment-coordination interfaces. The anchor concentration produces **the Philadelphia paradox** at SD4 (cell/gene therapy ATC concentration adjacent to populations most affected by SCD; G1-XC-05 = G1-SD4-01) and the specialty pharmacy access asymmetry at SD3 (G1-SD3-08).

**XC-11 pathway**: PA-3 constituent navigating small / independent organization architecture across SD1 cosmetics + SD2 ritual-slaughter retail + SD3 independent pharmacies + SD4 small clinical practices + SD5 small tobacco retailers + SD6 smaller harm-reduction organizations. The pattern is qualitatively different from federal-cluster or anchor-institution pathways — it operates at the upstream-vs-downstream layer asymmetry where structural pressures land at small / independent organization layer differentially against larger-organization infrastructure.

**Compound disadvantage geography pathway**: PA-3 constituent in N./Northwest Phila Core or portions of W. Phila Core or S. Phila experiencing simultaneous concentration of SD1 + SD2 + SD3 + SD4 + SD5 + SD6 documented patterns. The matrix above documents the simultaneous concentration; the pathway is the constituent-level cumulative effect of structural-pattern simultaneity at sub-area level.

