## §1. Material Changes from First-Pass Synthesis (Standard 11)

This section operationalizes Standard 11 at the synthesis-revision layer — explicit documentation of what changed between first-pass synthesis (RD2, 2026-04-28) and second-pass synthesis revision (RD3, 2026-04-29). Each change has a rationale and a documented anchor in the second-pass per-SD files or in Step 1 assessment / Step 2 prompts.

**Change 1 — "Mutually-reinforcing" → "structurally-co-occurring" at aggregate-finding cross-cutting through-line layer.** First-pass §2 framed the five cross-cutting through-lines as "five mutually-reinforcing cross-cutting through-lines." Second-pass replaces "mutually-reinforcing" with "structurally-co-occurring." Rationale: "mutually-reinforcing" implies a causal-direction relationship between the through-lines (each pattern strengthens the others); the documented evidence base supports observation of co-occurrence in PA-3 structural geography but does not support causal-direction claims at the inter-pattern layer. "Structurally-co-occurring" matches the underlying evidentiary commitment without overstating the inter-pattern relationship. This shift was identified at Step 1 assessment item 1.A and reaffirmed at Step 3 approval.

**Change 2 — "Three-vector MAHA-era federal direction" → "MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence" at SD7 / synthesis layer.** First-pass §1 Finding 7 and §2 Aggregate Finding framed federal direction as "three-vector MAHA-era direction" (rule withdrawals/vacaturs; MAHA priority pivots; industry-favorable substantive shifts). Second-pass replaces "three-vector federal direction" with "three-pattern co-occurrence." Rationale: "three-vector federal direction" implies a coherent MAHA program with three coordinated vectors of action; the documented evidence base supports observation of three patterns of federal action that have co-occurred in 2025-2026 but does not establish a coherent program with three coordinated vectors. The four-layer XC-9 timestamp discipline (announcement / effective / implementation / felt-reality) and the confabulation guard (rhetoric vs. implemented action vs. downstream effects) make the substitution methodologically necessary. Step 1 assessment item XC-F + Step 3 approval recommendation 1 anchor this change.

**Change 3 — Per-SD HACD / CNN / TCT sections added at second pass.** First-pass per-SD files used a 9-section template; the integrated 13-section template added Hierarchical Authority Chain Diagrams (HACD), Conversational Narrative Notes (CNN ≥4 paragraphs), and Temporal Change Tracking (TCT) at second pass. The second-pass per-SD files (SD1-SD7) all include these three sections. The synthesis-layer effects are: (a) HACD per-SD diagrams aggregate into the SD7 federal-cluster meta-diagram preserved in second-pass SD7; (b) CNN sections operationalize Standard 13 categorical compliance and Standard 8 representation-question framing in narrative form; (c) TCT sections operationalize XC-9 timestamp discipline at per-SD layer.

**Change 4 — XC-9 MAHA-attribution + timestamp discipline applied at per-SD and synthesis layers.** Each second-pass per-SD file includes timestamp resolution for MAHA-attributed items in stated categories. The synthesis-layer effect is documented at §6 below. Step 3 approval recommendation 1 anchors this change.

**Change 5 — XC-10 law-to-felt-reality notes file established as parallel scaffold.** A separate working notes file (D4_foodMed_law-to-felt-reality-notes_2026-04-29.md) tracks observations during second-pass execution about gaps between law-as-written and constituent-experienced-reality. The notes file is not a deliverable in the second-pass cycle; it is a forward-looking scaffold for project-lead later return. Six pattern types tracked (ITD / OAG / AEF / PRG / CCB / DII). Three initial observations plus per-SD entries appended during Step 4. Step 3 approval recommendation 2 anchors this change.

**Change 6 — XC-11 independent / small organization compliance + access asymmetry analysis surfaced new gap findings and access-asymmetry framings.** Each second-pass per-SD file examines differential regulatory burden + access asymmetry between large / conglomerated organizations and independent / small organizations. New gap findings surfaced: G1-SD2-07 (independent ritual-slaughter + small ethnic-market retail differential under split-jurisdiction); G1-SD4-07 (small clinical practices + small specialty practices differential vs. Penn / CHOP / Jefferson / Temple); F4-T2P2-SD1-MoCRA-1 (P2 verification target on small Black-owned cosmetics manufacturer MoCRA exemption-threshold reach). The synthesis-layer treatment is documented at §4 below. Step 3 approval recommendation 3 anchors this change.

**Change 7 — D4 cross-cutting findings refined; substantive infrastructure preserved.** The Compound Disadvantage Geography Matrix is preserved as central D4 finding-presentation infrastructure (referenced and developed across SD1, SD3, SD5, SD6, SD7 second-pass files). The "Philadelphia paradox" (G1-SD4-01 = G1-XC-05) is preserved as the defining D4 SD4 representation paradox. The IRA MFP / pharmacy desert intersection (G1-SD3-01, -02) is elevated as the defining D4 SD3 representation paradox in second-pass SD3. Refinements are detailed at §3 below.

**Change 8 — Carry-forward set expanded to D5 / D9 / D11 / D13 / D17 / D2.** First-pass §5 carried forward to D5, D9, D11, D17. Second-pass adds D13 (cross-domain compound-disadvantage geography overlay; pharmacy-desert geography as cross-domain access-architecture infrastructure) and D2 (vaccine policy litigation period; overdose mortality decline pattern; PA-3 chronic disease burden + pharmacy desert intersection — already established at first pass but feeding into D2 second-pass). The expansion is documented at §9 below.

