## §2. What This Domain Established — Updated Findings (1-7)

Updated numbered finding per sub-domain, plus the cross-cutting SD7 synthesis finding. These are the load-bearing substantive findings of D4 second-pass execution. Each updated finding refers to the corresponding second-pass per-SD file for full content; this section consolidates the synthesis-layer summary.

### Finding 1 (SD1 — Food)
The federal-state-local SD1 architecture (FFDCA Chapter IV; FSMA; DSHEA; FALCPA / FASTER; MoCRA; SNAP / WIC / NSLP; PA Food Safety Act; PADA; Philadelphia Code Title 6 Chapter 6-300 PDPH OFP; PDPH Get Healthy Philly + Healthy Corner Store Initiative + Food Bucks) provides comprehensive nominal protection but operates against a structural compound-disadvantage geography. Second-pass deepenings: HFP/OII reorganization (effective July 1, 2024) admin vulnerability rated HIGH; XC-9 timestamp resolution applied to USDA ERS termination + HFP/OII reorganization + April 2025 FDA RIF; XC-11 surfaced small Black-owned cosmetics manufacturer + MoCRA exemption-threshold reach question (NEW F4-T2P2-SD1-MoCRA-1 P2 verification target); G1-SD1-01 through 06 carried forward.

### Finding 2 (SD2 — Meat & Poultry)
The federal-state-local SD2 architecture (FMIA / PPIA / EPIA; PR/HACCP; FMIA § 1902 ritual-slaughter provision; Title 6 § 6-301 USDA jurisdictional carve-out at retail; PA Food Safety Act + PA Retail Food Facility Safety Act) operates with reduced federal-direct capacity at moment of structural concentration. Second-pass deepenings: April 25, 2025 Salmonella Framework withdrawal (90 FR 17344) elaborated as central material change; FSIS Philadelphia District 60 admin vulnerability rated MODERATE-HIGH; XC-11 surfaced G1-SD2-07 (independent ritual-slaughter + small ethnic-market retail differential under FMIA-vs-Title-6-§6-301 split-jurisdiction); cheaper-cut Salmonella exposure pathway concentrates on lower-income predominantly Black PA-3 consumer households after framework withdrawal.

### Finding 3 (SD3 — Drugs: CDER + CVM + IRA pricing)
The federal-state-local SD3 architecture (FFDCA Chapter V; PHSA § 351; Hatch-Waxman; BPCIA; PDUFA / GDUFA / BsUFA / OMUFA; IRA MFP; CSA; MAT Act; PA Act 77 of 2024 PBM reform; PA Pharmacy Act; PA Drug Act; ABC-MAP) executes substantive pricing-protection (IRA MFP first-round January 1, 2026) at exactly the moment when physical pharmacy access architecture in PA-3 collapses. Second-pass deepenings: IRA MFP / pharmacy desert intersection elevated as defining D4 SD3 representation paradox (G1-SD3-01, -02); CDER admin vulnerability rated HIGH (5-director sequence + April 2025 RIF); XC-11 independent pharmacies as load-bearing cross-cutting case (PBM reimbursement architecture + PA Act 77 differential effect + specialty pharmacy exclusion); CVM expanded; G1-SD3-01 through 08 carried forward.

### Finding 4 (SD4 — Biologics & Devices)
The federal-state-local SD4 architecture (PHSA § 351 + BPCIA; FFDCA § 513-§ 520; ACIP / Vaccines for Children; LDT framework — vacated; CMS Cell and Gene Therapy Access Model; PA Drug Act; PADoH school immunization; PDPH Division of Disease Control) carries the sharpest single representation paradox in D4. Second-pass deepenings: Philadelphia paradox (G1-SD4-01 = G1-XC-05) preserved as defining D4 SD4 representation paradox — Penn / CHOP cell/gene therapy ATCs in University City + ~90% Black SCD population + Casgevy ($2.2M) / Lyfgenia ($3.1M) low uptake; CBER + CDRH + ACIP simultaneous federal-layer flux = most volatile in D4; vaccine policy litigation timeline captured at TCT layer (June 2025 ACIP overhaul → October/November 2025 vaccine removals → January 5, 2026 schedule overhaul → March 2026 federal court block; prior schedule operative as of execution April 29, 2026); XC-11 surfaced G1-SD4-07 (small clinical practices + small specialty practices differential vs. Penn / CHOP / Jefferson / Temple).

### Finding 5 (SD5 — Tobacco)
The federal-state-local SD5 architecture (FFDCA Chapter IX TCA 2009; FDA CTP; PA Tobacco 21; PA CIAA; PA Tobacco Settlement Act; PA cigarette/tobacco product tax; Philadelphia Code Title 6 Chapter 6-700 + Title 10 Chapter 10-700; PDPH density cap 1 retailer per 1,000 + 500-foot school buffer; PDPH Get Healthy Philly) provides the most pointed industry-favorable substantive direction at moment of disparate-impact remediation availability in D4. Second-pass deepenings: menthol withdrawal RIN 0910-AI60 (January 2025) elaborated as defining industry-favorable-direction-at-disparate-impact-remediation-availability moment (G1-SD5-01); CTP admin vulnerability rated EXTREME (singular among D4 SDs; April 2025 Office of Regulations RIF — "entire office responsible for drafting new tobacco regulations" RIF'd); XC-11 small tobacco retailers under density cap + 500-foot school buffer differential burden vs. corporate-chain retailers; CNN centered on foreclosed-remediation pattern.

### Finding 6 (SD6 — Controlled Substances)
The federal-state-local SD6 architecture (CSA 1970; **today-effective DEA Final Order placing FDA-approved + state-licensed medical marijuana → Schedule III** — FR Doc. 2026-08176; effective April 29, 2026; 60-day priority window through June 22, 2026; MAT Act 2022 X-waiver elimination; SAMHSA OTP / SOR / SAPT; DEA telemedicine flexibilities through December 2026; PA Drug Act + PA Medical Marijuana Act 2016; ABC-MAP; PA opioid settlement disbursement; PDPH SUPHR; DBHIDS; **Mayor Parker $100M+ wellness ecosystem**) simultaneously delivers historic positive-direction trajectory and concentrates PA-3's sharpest single sub-area cumulative-disadvantage incidence. Second-pass deepenings: Today-effective DEA Schedule III timestamp captured at TCT layer; G1-SD6-02 careful framing on Black PA-3 OUD engagement pattern preserved (gap-finding without pathologizing parallel-infrastructure engagement); F4-T2P2-SD6-1 (P1 peer-reviewed Black-PA-3-OUD-population engagement-pattern lit) + F4-T2P2-SD6-2 (P1 DEA marijuana medical-licensee registration uptake first 60-day window) + F4-T2P2-SD6-4 (P2 medetomidine + xylazine + BTMPS chronic-illness lit); CNN on Kensington compound-incidence with both-true framing (overdose mortality decline ~24% from 2022 peak + Mayor Parker ecosystem expansion + structurally entrenched compound factors).

### Finding 7 (SD7 — Federal Regulatory Architecture: Synthesis) — REFRAMED
The cross-cutting D4 federal-regulatory architecture **operates under MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence** (rule withdrawals/vacaturs; capacity erosion; data-infrastructure rollback) — reframed from first-pass "three-vector federal direction" per Change 2 above. Five of six D4 sub-domains carry HIGH or EXTREME admin-vulnerability ratings (SD5 EXTREME; SD1, SD3, SD4 HIGH; SD2 MODERATE-HIGH; SD6 MODERATE). Second-pass deepenings: HACD synthesizes per-SD chains into federal-cluster meta-diagram; Compound Disadvantage Geography Matrix preserved as central D4 finding-presentation infrastructure; G1-SD7-01 framing tightened to "three-pattern co-occurrence"; F4-T2P2-SD7-1 (P1 federal capacity quantification by Center post-RIF Q1-Q2 2026) + F4-T2P2-SD7-2 (P1 USDA ERS termination scope) flagged as P1 verification targets.

