## §10. Domain-Level Representation Question (Standard 8) — refined

**What the legal framework provides or promises**: Preserved from first-pass §6 with no substantive change. D4's federal-state-local architecture is the most comprehensive of any domain in this representation analysis project.

**What PA-3 constituents actually receive**: Preserved from first-pass §6 with second-pass refinements at the per-SD layer (see §2 above for updated findings).

**What accounts for the gap**: Refined per Change 2 — federal direction in 2025-2026 systematically forecloses or substantively redirects regulatory tools at the precise moments when documented disparate-impact remediation was operationally available. **MAHA-era three-pattern co-occurrence** (rule withdrawals + capacity erosion + data-infrastructure rollback) — reframed from "MAHA-era three-vector pattern" — operates simultaneously at PA-3 layer. Compound disadvantage geography overlay produces structurally entrenched sub-area receipt asymmetry. Pharmacy desert post-Rite-Aid intersects multiple sub-domains. Cell/gene therapy "Philadelphia paradox" reflects structural barriers operating against geographic proximity. Kensington compound-incidence locus concentrates SD1 + SD3 + SD5 + SD6 incidence simultaneously and interacts with housing + criminalization + healthcare access cross-cuts. **Second-pass addition**: Independent / small organization compliance + access asymmetry constitutes a sixth structurally-co-occurring pattern at the receipt-architecture layer (XC-11; §4 above).

**What the gap means for the representation analysis**: Preserved from first-pass §6 with second-pass refinement on representation-question framing. D4's architecture provides identical formal access for all PA-3 constituents; structural features produce divergent actual receipt. The gap is a function of (a) what the formal architecture *does not* address; (b) what the formal architecture *was about to address* and then withdrew; (c) what the data infrastructure *characterizes less and less well*; (d) what the geographic compound-disadvantage overlay *structurally produces*; **second-pass addition**: (e) what the regulatory architecture's compliance + reimbursement + tool-withdrawal effects fall asymmetrically on smaller / independent organizations — affecting access pathway for PA-3 constituents in stated categories who rely on smaller / independent organizations more than on large conglomerated entities. The D4 representation question is uniquely complex within this representation analysis project: the architecture that could substantively address documented PA-3 disparities is operationally retreating from doing so at the moment of sharpest documented compound-incidence pattern in lower-income predominantly Black sub-areas. The representation gap in D4 falls sharpest on Black PA-3 constituents in North/Northwest Philadelphia Core and Cobbs Creek-area West Philadelphia Core sub-areas — the precise populations the federal regulatory tools would have most substantively benefited and the populations most affected by tool withdrawal, capacity erosion, and data-infrastructure rollback.

---

